1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,080
So in this case, if we reverse the order,

2
00:00:02,080 --> 00:00:05,040
then we do have no x-direct equilibrium.

3
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:07,840
And then the game-theoretical prediction

4
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:10,200
is people do whatever they were going to do in this turn,

5
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:11,160
and then they were going to do whatever

6
00:00:11,160 --> 00:00:13,480
they would do in this game, and then they

7
00:00:13,480 --> 00:00:15,100
do whatever they would do in that game.

8
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:17,360
And the fact that the games are snapped together

9
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:18,400
doesn't change anything.

10
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:23,920
OK, so questions about this?

11
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:24,600
Yes?

12
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:28,320
So just to cement that last fact that you were just making,

13
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:32,520
so basically there's no way, no enforcement or punishment

14
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:33,240
that you would do?

15
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:33,720
Right.

16
00:00:33,720 --> 00:00:35,520
Yeah, so if the same thing always happens,

17
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:37,360
then you can't threaten people with anything.

18
00:00:37,360 --> 00:00:39,520
On the other hand, if there's multiple equilibria,

19
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:41,880
then you actually do have a threat available, right?

20
00:00:41,880 --> 00:00:43,760
Because you could say, well, if that happens,

21
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:46,120
we'll go to the other equilibria.

22
00:00:46,120 --> 00:00:48,760
And you say, well, why would you do that?

23
00:00:48,760 --> 00:00:50,760
It is our way.

24
00:00:50,760 --> 00:00:53,360
It is our custom that that is what occurs.

25
00:00:53,360 --> 00:00:55,800
That is what we expect.

26
00:00:55,800 --> 00:00:58,160
And again, if you think about real life,

27
00:00:58,160 --> 00:01:00,000
you may notice that there are circumstances

28
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,040
where people go to bad equilibria very naturally.

29
00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:05,120
In the real world, there's probably more psychology

30
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:05,880
going on.

31
00:01:05,880 --> 00:01:09,520
But suppose that your neighbor goes and blows up

32
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:11,520
your mailbox.

33
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:15,320
Now, after the other, you say, well, look,

34
00:01:15,320 --> 00:01:16,560
I've blown up one mailbox.

35
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:19,800
There's no reason we can't still be friends now.

36
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:22,960
Look, wouldn't it be better if you lost a mailbox

37
00:01:22,960 --> 00:01:24,600
but kept a friend?

38
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:26,400
And that might seem like a reasonable thing,

39
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:29,480
but you have a pretty good idea that's not what would occur.

40
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:32,760
And instead, maybe you'll blow up his mailbox.

41
00:01:32,760 --> 00:01:35,400
And notice, there's actually something very focal about,

42
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:36,720
he blew up my mailbox.

43
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:38,760
I will blow up his mailbox.

44
00:01:38,760 --> 00:01:41,640
Whereas, just in terms of logical retaliation,

45
00:01:41,640 --> 00:01:44,760
it might be that I will go and break one of his windows

46
00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:46,160
or I'll slash his tires.

47
00:01:46,160 --> 00:01:48,200
There's all kinds of other kinds of payback

48
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:49,480
that you could get.

49
00:01:49,480 --> 00:01:51,000
But it's very focal to think about,

50
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:53,280
I will do exactly the same bad thing to him

51
00:01:53,280 --> 00:01:54,480
that he did to me.

52
00:01:54,480 --> 00:01:57,560
I for I. Yes, I for I. This is very, very good.

53
00:01:57,560 --> 00:02:01,560
So I think you may wonder, why is that so focal?

54
00:02:01,560 --> 00:02:05,760
It could be that at least one common story about the I to I

55
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:09,280
norm is that it was intended to limit overreaction, where you

56
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:11,960
go and murder someone for I. So if you just say,

57
00:02:11,960 --> 00:02:14,280
look, you can do to him what he did to you,

58
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:17,600
but you can't do anything more.

59
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:20,880
All right, so questions about finite repetition

60
00:02:20,880 --> 00:02:23,760
and how that might change things.

61
00:02:23,760 --> 00:02:24,960
OK.

62
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:26,640
All right, so that's finite repetition.

63
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:29,520
Now, have you ever heard that finite repetition doesn't

64
00:02:29,520 --> 00:02:30,200
change anything?

65
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:33,400
That's actually correct, as I've just shown you.

66
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:36,000
And I'm pretty sure that several of you,

67
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,320
if there's a question about this on the conference and exam,

68
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:43,720
and the question says, true, false, and explain,

69
00:02:43,720 --> 00:02:47,120
finite repetition never changes the outcomes for the game,

70
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:49,320
I'm pretty sure there's going to be several of you who

71
00:02:49,320 --> 00:02:51,000
will immediately get the wrong answer.

72
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:52,800
Of course, if I give you the test right now,

73
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:54,160
you don't get the right answer.

74
00:02:54,160 --> 00:02:55,720
But, right?

75
00:02:55,720 --> 00:02:59,320
And that is because people remember

76
00:02:59,320 --> 00:03:03,920
the garbled, confused half-truth of what's going on,

77
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:06,520
rather than the actual lesson, which

78
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:08,480
is, of course, what we don't want to remember,

79
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:11,080
is the garbled, confused half-truth.

80
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:13,880
So it's all notes.

81
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:18,120
All right, so anyway, so even in finite repetitive games,

82
00:03:18,120 --> 00:03:20,160
the set of Nash equilibria expands,

83
00:03:20,160 --> 00:03:22,560
or is what theorists say, weakly expands.

84
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:24,800
So it either stays the same or gets bigger.

85
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:26,200
Both things can happen.

86
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:29,320
All right, but expands less than you might think.

87
00:03:29,320 --> 00:03:29,840
How so?

88
00:03:29,840 --> 00:03:33,360
Well, imagine you repeat the prisoner's slum a hundred times.

89
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:35,520
All right, could you sink cooperation

90
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:37,640
by threatening retaliation?

91
00:03:37,640 --> 00:03:40,880
Right, and here we come to a classic argument

92
00:03:40,880 --> 00:03:44,200
saying that the answer, strangely, is no.

93
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:46,080
Right, and here's the argument.

94
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:48,800
Well, what's going to happen in turn 100?

95
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:49,720
Somebody betrays.

96
00:03:49,720 --> 00:03:52,040
Yes, well, in fact, both will betray, right?

97
00:03:52,040 --> 00:03:55,120
Both will betray in turn 100 because there's no tomorrow.

98
00:03:55,120 --> 00:03:57,400
There's not, so like in turn 100, if you say,

99
00:03:57,400 --> 00:03:59,800
if you go and betray me, I'll get back at you.

100
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:00,400
How?

101
00:04:00,400 --> 00:04:02,560
It's the last turn of the game.

102
00:04:02,560 --> 00:04:05,640
There's no other opportunity for you to get revenge on me.

103
00:04:05,640 --> 00:04:08,320
So you're not going to cooperate in turn 100, and I guess I'm

104
00:04:08,320 --> 00:04:09,320
not.

105
00:04:09,320 --> 00:04:11,000
Oh, whoops, I said it, right?

106
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:13,280
I shouldn't have said that I wasn't going to cooperate.

107
00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:15,560
Maybe if you had done that, I could have tricked you.

108
00:04:15,560 --> 00:04:17,200
All right, so then here's the problem.

109
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:20,920
Suppose in turn 99 you say, if you betray me in turn 99,

110
00:04:20,920 --> 00:04:22,720
I'll betray you in turn 100.

111
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:24,120
Now, here's the interesting thing.

112
00:04:24,120 --> 00:04:26,360
Is it true that if a person betrays you in turn 99,

113
00:04:26,360 --> 00:04:28,960
you'll betray them in turn 100?

114
00:04:28,960 --> 00:04:29,800
Necessarily.

115
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:32,640
Well, sure, because you're going to betray them in turn 100

116
00:04:32,640 --> 00:04:34,240
no matter what they do.

117
00:04:34,240 --> 00:04:38,480
So if you say, if A, I will betray you, right,

118
00:04:38,480 --> 00:04:42,440
and if you will always betray, then if A betray, it's true.

119
00:04:42,440 --> 00:04:43,840
Right?

120
00:04:43,840 --> 00:04:44,920
Make sense?

121
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:46,400
All right, so but here's the thing.

122
00:04:46,400 --> 00:04:49,200
If you threaten to do something that you will always do,

123
00:04:49,200 --> 00:04:51,560
how scary is that?

124
00:04:51,560 --> 00:04:53,880
Or at least how motivating is it?

125
00:04:53,880 --> 00:04:54,720
Right?

126
00:04:54,720 --> 00:04:59,720
So if you know you're getting executed anyway for a crime,

127
00:04:59,720 --> 00:05:02,240
and then someone says, if you don't speak respectfully

128
00:05:02,240 --> 00:05:05,480
to the executioner, I'll execute you.

129
00:05:05,480 --> 00:05:07,920
You're executing me anyway.

130
00:05:07,920 --> 00:05:10,360
And of course, then in a really brutal system,

131
00:05:10,360 --> 00:05:12,200
they say, well, we might torture you first.

132
00:05:12,200 --> 00:05:14,960
Oh, whoops, OK, I'll shut up.

133
00:05:14,960 --> 00:05:17,800
But if you know they're already doing the worst possible thing

134
00:05:17,800 --> 00:05:22,440
to you, then in a way, you are free.

135
00:05:22,440 --> 00:05:23,120
Right?

136
00:05:23,120 --> 00:05:25,760
Whatever you do, whatever I do, you're

137
00:05:25,760 --> 00:05:28,840
doing the same thing to me, so why do I care what you threaten?

138
00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:30,880
Ah.

139
00:05:30,880 --> 00:05:33,400
So in turn 99, if you know that your opponent's definitely

140
00:05:33,400 --> 00:05:35,520
going to betray you no matter what you do,

141
00:05:35,520 --> 00:05:37,360
they have no threat to hang over your head,

142
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:39,680
so you may as well betray in turn 99.

143
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:41,640
Then in turn 98, you know that you're

144
00:05:41,640 --> 00:05:44,480
going to get betrayed in turn 99, so why cooperate then?

145
00:05:44,480 --> 00:05:46,840
Turn 97, turn 96.

146
00:05:46,840 --> 00:05:49,320
And if you follow the logic back,

147
00:05:49,320 --> 00:05:53,040
the result seems to be that you won't even cooperate in turn 1.

148
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:54,600
And it seems weird for 100 turns.

149
00:05:54,600 --> 00:05:58,000
How weird is it if it's a game where a million turns long?

150
00:05:58,000 --> 00:05:59,440
Where you say, well, I know you're

151
00:05:59,440 --> 00:06:01,200
going to betray me in turn 1 million,

152
00:06:01,200 --> 00:06:04,520
so I'm going to betray you in turn 1.

153
00:06:04,520 --> 00:06:08,920
Anyone's parent would say, just cooperate with him for a while.

154
00:06:08,920 --> 00:06:11,200
It's probably going to be a lot better than getting

155
00:06:11,200 --> 00:06:13,040
into a big fight in the beginning.

156
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:14,440
You don't understand game theory.

157
00:06:14,440 --> 00:06:16,560
This is a hopeless situation, and I

158
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:20,040
may as well protect myself by betraying him

159
00:06:20,040 --> 00:06:21,680
right from the very outset.

160
00:06:26,080 --> 00:06:28,080
So if you push the logic back, then you

161
00:06:28,080 --> 00:06:32,240
get to this odd result that no one will cooperate ever

162
00:06:32,240 --> 00:06:34,760
in the finitely repeated prisoner's lemma.

163
00:06:34,760 --> 00:06:36,400
And there's even a special name for it.

164
00:06:36,400 --> 00:06:38,320
We call it unraveling.

165
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:39,680
We call it unraveling.

166
00:06:39,680 --> 00:06:41,880
This is a metaphor that you may have experienced

167
00:06:41,880 --> 00:06:43,920
when you were a kid and you had a string hanging

168
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:44,840
from a sweater.

169
00:06:44,840 --> 00:06:47,200
And you decided the best way to solve the problem

170
00:06:47,200 --> 00:06:48,800
would be to pull the string.

171
00:06:48,800 --> 00:06:51,840
And if you pull it and pull it and pull it and pull it,

172
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:54,880
and finally you have just a big pile of thread,

173
00:06:54,880 --> 00:06:57,120
and your sweater is gone.

174
00:06:57,120 --> 00:07:01,040
Pull, pull, pull, and the whole thing falls to pieces.

175
00:07:01,040 --> 00:07:04,160
So that's how people often describe a game like this.

176
00:07:04,160 --> 00:07:10,320
They will say it unravels to a totally uncooperative outcome.

177
00:07:10,320 --> 00:07:14,480
Now, how does the finitely repeated prisoner's lemma

178
00:07:14,480 --> 00:07:17,080
differ from this game here?

179
00:07:17,080 --> 00:07:22,320
So notice, this game seems like it wouldn't be good enough,

180
00:07:22,320 --> 00:07:23,920
wouldn't be enough to say cooperation.

181
00:07:23,920 --> 00:07:25,040
It's just like two games.

182
00:07:25,040 --> 00:07:26,540
Like, we play two times, and that's

183
00:07:26,540 --> 00:07:27,880
going to change everything.

184
00:07:27,880 --> 00:07:29,480
On the other hand, the prisoner's lemma

185
00:07:29,480 --> 00:07:32,200
with 100 turns, that's seemingly like 100 turns.

186
00:07:32,200 --> 00:07:34,320
How could that not make a difference?

187
00:07:34,320 --> 00:07:35,840
And yet, actually, here we've seen

188
00:07:35,840 --> 00:07:37,480
there is a way of getting cooperation

189
00:07:37,480 --> 00:07:39,400
using standard game theory.

190
00:07:39,400 --> 00:07:43,440
But with a 100-turn prisoner's lemma,

191
00:07:43,440 --> 00:07:45,720
the theorem is that there's no way of actually getting

192
00:07:45,720 --> 00:07:48,160
cooperation.

193
00:07:48,160 --> 00:07:49,400
So what's the difference?

194
00:07:49,400 --> 00:07:50,880
The difference is that here we end

195
00:07:50,880 --> 00:07:53,760
with a game with two equilibria.

196
00:07:53,760 --> 00:07:56,800
We end with a game with two equilibria, with more than one.

197
00:07:56,800 --> 00:08:01,600
And once you've got more than one, then you've got a threat.

198
00:08:01,600 --> 00:08:04,560
Then the person doesn't know that the same thing

199
00:08:04,560 --> 00:08:06,060
will happen no matter what they do.

200
00:08:06,060 --> 00:08:07,640
Maybe something different will happen,

201
00:08:07,640 --> 00:08:08,720
depending upon what I do.

202
00:08:08,720 --> 00:08:10,360
And if I know that your treatment of me

203
00:08:10,360 --> 00:08:12,560
depends upon how I treated you in the past,

204
00:08:12,560 --> 00:08:15,320
there's at least a reason for me to think twice about doing

205
00:08:15,320 --> 00:08:17,320
something that's bad for you.

206
00:08:17,320 --> 00:08:20,760
I have to consider how you will respond.

207
00:08:20,760 --> 00:08:24,720
So in this game, there's a kind of revenge that's available.

208
00:08:24,720 --> 00:08:27,080
You can't go and commit the kind of revenge

209
00:08:27,080 --> 00:08:29,240
which is maximally bad for me.

210
00:08:29,240 --> 00:08:31,120
Because again, the maximally bad thing for me

211
00:08:31,120 --> 00:08:34,440
would just be to do the opposite of what I'm going to do.

212
00:08:34,440 --> 00:08:38,000
But that is a case where you would be just hurting yourself.

213
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:41,000
But on the other hand, switching over

214
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:44,040
to a less favorable equilibrium for both of us,

215
00:08:44,040 --> 00:08:46,680
that's something actually that is believable.

216
00:08:46,680 --> 00:08:48,520
Because if I believe that you believe

217
00:08:48,520 --> 00:08:52,040
that I'm going to do it, I want to actually go through with it.

218
00:08:52,040 --> 00:08:54,040
I want to actually go through with it,

219
00:08:54,040 --> 00:08:56,520
which is the key difference.

220
00:08:56,520 --> 00:09:00,640
So this game allows a sort of revenge,

221
00:09:00,640 --> 00:09:03,120
whereas the finite interpreter prisoner's level really

222
00:09:03,120 --> 00:09:05,080
does not.

223
00:09:05,080 --> 00:09:09,080
What about the financial repeated coordination game?

224
00:09:09,080 --> 00:09:10,200
Oh, well, that's fine.

225
00:09:10,200 --> 00:09:12,160
I mean, in fact, a one-shot coordination game,

226
00:09:12,160 --> 00:09:14,520
you can get the best result.

227
00:09:14,520 --> 00:09:18,360
So yeah, of course, if you're mean at it,

228
00:09:18,360 --> 00:09:21,480
since you can get the best result with one turn,

229
00:09:21,480 --> 00:09:24,560
you can definitely get the best result with more turns,

230
00:09:24,560 --> 00:09:27,520
which is a basic theorem that if something

231
00:09:27,520 --> 00:09:30,400
is an equilibrium for each game to consider separately,

232
00:09:30,400 --> 00:09:32,320
it's also always an equilibrium.

233
00:09:32,320 --> 00:09:34,920
It is one equilibrium for the game considered as a whole.

234
00:09:34,920 --> 00:09:40,760
So where does it end?

235
00:09:40,760 --> 00:09:42,920
So if the game is dominant solvable, or in general,

236
00:09:42,920 --> 00:09:45,680
if there's only one equilibrium in the last game,

237
00:09:45,680 --> 00:09:47,480
then there's no way of threatening anything

238
00:09:47,480 --> 00:09:50,080
in that last game.

239
00:09:50,080 --> 00:09:53,040
Now, remember, in actual experiments

240
00:09:53,040 --> 00:09:55,520
where real humans play these games,

241
00:09:55,520 --> 00:09:57,160
it turns out that people do cooperate

242
00:09:57,160 --> 00:09:59,160
in finite interpreter games to a greater extent

243
00:09:59,160 --> 00:09:59,960
than one-shot games.

244
00:09:59,960 --> 00:10:00,460
OK.

245
00:10:04,640 --> 00:10:07,000
So there's some attempts at theoretically model this.

246
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:08,800
So the guy who's mentioning economists

247
00:10:08,800 --> 00:10:12,560
was Reinhard Zeltmann, who won the Nobel Prize.

248
00:10:12,560 --> 00:10:16,720
And the basic story that he set up is something like this.

249
00:10:16,720 --> 00:10:19,120
Suppose that you and I are both playing prisoner's level

250
00:10:19,120 --> 00:10:19,760
for 100 turns.

251
00:10:19,760 --> 00:10:21,040
We both know this.

252
00:10:21,040 --> 00:10:24,480
But there's one thing that we don't both know for sure.

253
00:10:24,480 --> 00:10:27,760
And that is whether the opponent understands the game.

254
00:10:27,760 --> 00:10:29,300
I know if I understand the game, but I

255
00:10:29,300 --> 00:10:31,640
don't know for sure if you understand the game.

256
00:10:31,640 --> 00:10:34,520
So suppose that each of us says there's a 10% chance

257
00:10:34,520 --> 00:10:37,240
that our opponent doesn't really understand the game.

258
00:10:37,240 --> 00:10:40,120
In that case, it could be totally in your self-interest

259
00:10:40,120 --> 00:10:41,920
to cooperate in turn one.

260
00:10:41,920 --> 00:10:43,960
Because if you cooperate in turn one,

261
00:10:43,960 --> 00:10:46,960
this creates doubt in the mind of your opponent

